les was the most celebrated painter of ancient Greece, active in the 4th century BCE.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/apennines"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/apennines.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Apennines</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Apennines are the mountain range that runs about 1,200 kilometres down the length of the Italian peninsula, from Liguria to Calabria.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/apepi"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/apepi.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Apepi</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Apepi was a Hyksos king of the Fifteenth Dynasty of Egypt who ruled from Avaris in the eastern Delta for some forty years in the 16th century BCE, the best-attested…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/aphra-behn"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/aphra-behn.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Aphra Behn</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Aphra Behn (1640–1689) was an English playwright, poet and novelist, one of the first Englishwomen to earn a living by writing.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/aphsad-inscription"><span class="enc-name">Aphsad inscription</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Aphsad inscription is a Sanskrit stone inscription of the 7th century found at Aphsad, near Nawada in Bihar, India.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/apia"><span class="enc-name">Apia</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Apia is the capital and largest town of Samoa, on the north coast of Upolu.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/apil-sin"